## Summary: Making Invisible Work Visible

Internal platform updates—like those to a Drupal content management system (CMS)—rarely make the staff meeting. But their impact on an organization can be profound. This case study details how I transformed a routine Drupal update into a compelling story of organizational value.

By shifting the conversation from technical "maintenance" to strategic "mission," I demonstrated that our team's work wasn't just about keeping the lights on. It was about **reclaiming an estimated 58 hours per month** for the editorial team, empowering them to focus on content strategy instead of manual, time-consuming tasks.

This is a repeatable framework I use to turn invisible wins into a tangible investments in the company's core mission.

---

## The Challenge: The Paradox of Internal Tools

The value of a well-functioning internal tool or CMS is often invisible. The better it works, the less anyone notices it. This creates a paradox:

* The work is vital, yet it goes unseen.
* Its value lies in avoided pain and increased efficiency, not flashy new features.
* Leadership often sees the cost of the platform but not the daily friction it removes.

To overcome this, I recognized the need to actively tell the story of how my technical work benefited the entire organization, not just the development team. My goal was to translate a technical stat—a faster menu load time—into a strategic win: **58 hours of reclaimed staff time.**

---

## The Framework: From Technical Task to Strategic Story

I used a simple, three-part framework to communicate the value of the teams recent Drupal update. This approach can be applied to nearly any internal technical project.

### 1. Quantify the Pain to Amplify the Gain

Before writing a single line of code, I identified a key point of user friction. For the editorial team, a clunky, nine-step process was required to create a cover image for a PDF. This "Photoshop shuffle" was a significant time sink.

I documented the "before" state and used it to calculate the potential "after." The team didn’t just fix a bug; we saved a specific amount of time per task, which added up to a massive aggregate win: **58 hours a month.**

### 2. Connect the Win to the Mission

A number alone isn't enough. The most critical step was tying my technical work back to the organization’s core purpose. The organization’s mission is to create high-quality content that serves its audience. Less time spent on manual image crops meant more time for editors to create, refine, and strategize content.

The Drupal update wasn't a cost center; it was a **capacity multiplier** that directly invested in the organization's ability to execute its mission.

### 3. Share the Story Widely and Simply

The aim is to communicate this value in a clear, accessible way to key stakeholders, avoiding technical jargon and led with the most important message: the time savings.

Here is a condensed, anonymized version of the email I sent to leadership:

> **Subject:** Latest platform release reclaims 1.5 work weeks per month for editors
>
> Hi Team,
>
> The recent platform release delivered tangible improvements that reduce friction for editors and elevate content quality.
>
> The headline: These changes reclaim an estimated **58 hours per month.** This effectively gifts the organization 1.5 extra work weeks each month to reinvest in strategic initiatives. That’s the power of platform standardization: less friction, more time for mission-critical work.
>
> **Key Example: Document Processing**
>
> **Before: 9 steps** – often inconsistent and error-prone
>
> 1.  Create entry for PDF
> 2.  Open Photoshop
> 3.  Crop PDF cover
> 4.  Save file
> 5.  Create media entry for PDF cover
> 6.  Upload image of PDF cover
> 7.  Name entry
> 8.  Specify 6 different image crops
> 9.  Select image for PDF cover
>
> **After: 3 steps**
>
> 1.  Create entry for PDF
> 2.  System automatically generates an image of the PDF cover
> 3.  Select image, overriding if desired

This simple before-and-after example made the relief and value tangible for everyone, regardless of their technical expertise.

